#### Investigating the Compromised Windows 10 Client

Let’s assume that you have followed the appropriate processes to collect the compromised Windows image. You will now look for indications of compromise. An Intrusion Detection System (IDS) alert notified the network security staff that a machine with the Internet Protocol (IP) address of 192.168.1.10 had been compromised. The alert on the machine was related to Server Message Block (SMB). The machine was unplugged and imaged on April 4, 2020.

There are many places to look for evidence of an intrusion on a Windows system, including the following:

- Event Viewer files
    - The Windows Event Viewer shows a log of application and system message including errors, information, and warnings in Windows.
- Registry files
    - The Windows Registry is an extensive database of user and application settings on a Windows system.
- Startup folder
    - The Windows Startup folder stores the applications that will be launched by Windows when Windows boots.
- Folders in the path C:\Windows and C:\Windows\System32
    - These folders are where the majority of the Windows operating system files are located.
- Scheduled Tasks folder
    - The Tasks folder is where Windows stores the automated tasks to run when triggered.

In this lab, you will examine and analyze different parts of the Windows machine to uncover evidence of compromise.



